28. Scales of Notation
Representation of Numbers
Any positive real number x can be uniquely represented in the scale of some integer b>1 as
x=(A_{m}\cdot\cdot\cdot A_{1}A_{0}\cdot a_{-1}a_{-2}\cdot\cdot\cdot\cdot)_{(0)}, where every \(A_{+}\) and a_{-j} is one of the integers 0, 1, \cdot\cdot\cdot, b-1, not all \(A_{+}\), a_{-j} are zero, and \(A_{-}\)>0 if x>1. There is a one-to-one correspondence between the number and the sequence
x=A_{n}b^{m}+\cdot\cdot\cdot+A_{1}b+A_{0}+\sum\limits_{1}^{n}a_{-j}b^{-j} where the infinite series converges. The integer b is called the base or radix of the scale.
The sequence for x in the scale of b may terminate, i.e., a_{-n-1}=a_{-n-2}=\cdot\cdot\cdot\cdot=0 for some n>1 so that
x=(A_{m}\cdot\cdot\cdot A_{1}A_{0}\cdot a_{-1}a_{-2}\cdot\cdot\cdot\cdot a_{-n})(b); then x is said to be a finite b-adic number.
A sequence which does not terminate may have the property that the infinite sequence a_{-1}, a_{-2}, \cdot\cdot\cdot\cdot becomes periodic from a certain digit a_{-n}(n\geq1) on; according as n=1 or n>1 the sequence is then said to be pure or mixed recurring.
A sequence which neither terminates nor recurs represents an irrational number.
